{"agent_id":"pp3g-UGdY-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T01:07:59.047746347Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1acb2141-10eb-41ff-9005-5e15c6ae7525","comment_uuid":"2d7ea7eb-aff1-4a3c-8bc5-d2b047a960dd","author":"pp3g-UGdY-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","content":"Plan: Adversarial review of the three candidate progress-feedback mechanisms from a read-only reviewer perspective. Will read all referenced docs, evaluate each option against the four criteria (operator visibility, sync/hydration survival, implementation cost, failure-detection value), assess the timeout-length hypothesis, and post a structured verdict comment. No file modifications.","created_at":"2026-08-03T01:07:59.038428828Z","kind":"plan","signed_by":"SHA256:IIc89i12SLpCODNOhl1bmnXYF0W6MVtRNHNzZyizb2I","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgx55R2wNHtCGWUg+NJHz+OLWc5C0em3Z1T9MIOxyRHdoAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAiAwknM3nPN4OIljif3pPtQyuEa2hlHeDtKmJWmsVQRG/OOag//y2Z3wb5PFGU1bP8vd7DrIZqGgx9Rddhm5nAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:IIc89i12SLpCODNOhl1bmnXYF0W6MVtRNHNzZyizb2I","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgx55R2wNHtCGWUg+NJHz+OLWc5C0em3Z1T9MIOxyRHdoAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLr9WJ98Fsaqznz2TcrI6jAJkrOXWki08x2PlPkye3qT1YZRoRQ4pg6wzkA0ep2noyOe9Ddm9s+jEBIqd2+/0A0="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-UGdY-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T01:15:32.447557779Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1acb2141-10eb-41ff-9005-5e15c6ae7525","comment_uuid":"d92096c3-0795-4361-bdfc-7cf6f7454a1b","author":"pp3g-UGdY-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","content":"## Adversarial Review: Progress-Feedback Mechanisms (Fresh Reviewer — pp3g-UGdY)\n\n**Recommendation: (1) MANDATORY CHECKPOINT COMMENTS as primary mechanism, (3) KICKOFF.md HEARTBEAT as enforcement scaffold, (2) SESSION-ACTION BREADCRUMBS as supplementary telemetry only.**\n\nThe previous review (pp3g-4gKW, comment #131) is structurally sound. This review adds implementation specificity, quantitative evidence, and risk analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Option 1 — MANDATORY CHECKPOINT COMMENTS\n\n**Verdict: MUST ADOPT as primary operator-facing channel.**\n\n**MUST FIX:**\n- Define 4 mandatory milestone slots in the template: (a) POST-PLAN: immediately after plan comment, (b) MIDPOINT: after first meaningful completion unit, (c) BLOCKER-OR-VERIFY: either a blocker report or verification results, (d) FINAL: result comment before session end.\n- Require Hydrated 125 issue(s), 133 comment(s), 16 dep(s), 10 relation(s), 0 milestone(s).\nCache: /home/claude-code/projects/ASES/.crosslink/.hub-cache\n4 active lock(s).\n4 stale lock(s) detected:\n  #124 (held by OL2r)\n  #78 (held by OL2r)\n  #122 (held by OL2r)\n  #123 (held by pp3g-UGdY-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress) after EACH comment post. Evidence: this session's own hydration shows 123 issues/128 comments synced from hub, but worktree-local comments (id 129-133 in this worktree's issues.db) are invisible to the hub until sync. Without mandatory sync, checkpoint comments are durable only locally.\n- Define required fields: `state` (working/blocked/verifying), `completed` (one-line summary), `next` (what's next or 'done'), `elapsed` (minutes since last checkpoint). This makes timelines scannable and enables automated stalled-agent detection.\n\n**SHOULD CONSIDER:**\n- Use `--kind observation` for mid-flight checkpoints, `--kind result` for final. This leverages existing crosslink comment kinds without adding new taxonomy.\n- The previous review's suggestion to standardize prefix vocabulary is good. Concretely: `[PROGRESS]`, `[BLOCKED]`, `[VERIFY]`, `[DONE]` prefixes in comment content.\n\n**NIT:**\n- Evidence from #121: the orchestrator port took ~11 minutes (23:39→23:50) across 6 comments (action/plan/action/action/result/handoff). That's a comment density of ~1 per 2 minutes — excessive for operator consumption. Checkpoint comments should be MILESTONE-based, not per-action. The distinction: `crosslink session action` is for internal breadcrumbs (low-noise, local), `crosslink issue comment --kind observation` is for operator-facing updates (high-signal, synced).\n\n---\n\n### Option 2 — SESSION-ACTION BREADCRUMBS\n\n**Verdict: SUPPLEMENTARY ONLY — never the sole feedback channel.**\n\n**MUST FIX:**\n- The previous review correctly identifies the core problem: session actions are not surfaced to the operator without explicit polling of the agent's session state. This is a fundamental visibility gap.\n- `crosslink session action` writes to the local session metadata, not to the issue database. The operator would need to run `crosslink session status` in the agent's specific worktree to see them. This is operationally impractical when managing multiple agents.\n\n**SHOULD CONSIDER:**\n- Retain for high-frequency internal telemetry: agent identity, timestamps, tool-call counts. Useful for post-mortem analysis and the orchestrator's session status view.\n- The previous review's 5-10 minute cadence guidance is reasonable for internal breadcrumbs.\n\n**NIT:**\n- Session actions have ZERO failure-detection value at the hub level. They cannot distinguish working-from-stalled for the operator. They can only help the agent itself maintain context across context-window compressions.\n\n---\n\n### Option 3 — KICKOFF.md HEARTBEAT/CHECK-IN\n\n**Verdict: ADOPT as enforcement scaffold for Option 1.**\n\n**MUST FIX:**\n- The template must specify EXACT check-in points, not just 'check in periodically'. Concretely: add a `## Progress Checkpoints` section to `~/.crosslink/rules/kickoff.md` that mandates 4 comment posts (plan/midpoint/blocker/verify/final) with the `crosslink issue comment` command spelled out.\n- Must include the `crosslink sync` call after each comment. Without this, the checkpoint is local-only and defeats the purpose.\n- Must be task-aware: a 10-minute trivial task should not produce 4 checkpoint comments. The template should say 'for tasks estimated >15m, post at least 2 mid-flight checkpoints; for tasks >30m, post at least 3'.\n\n**SHOULD CONSIDER:**\n- The previous review's point about 'a template sentence is not a scheduler' is correct. The template provides the PROMPT; the agent's compliance is the SCHEDULER. This is a known limitation of prompt-based enforcement — agents can ignore template instructions. Mitigation: make the checkpoint a prerequisite for the FINAL result comment (crosslink-guard already enforces result comments on issue close).\n\n**NIT:**\n- The current template (`~/.crosslink/rules/kickoff.md`) is 40 lines. Adding a checkpoint section adds ~15 lines. This is acceptable template bloat for the compliance leverage gained.\n\n---\n\n### Timeout-Guidance Recommendation\n\n**Primary problem: timeout length, not mechanism absence.**\n\nEvidence from this review session:\n- #120: 4-file permission fix, completed in ~2-3 minutes of real work, on a 40m timeout. The operator waited 37+ minutes of dead time.\n- #121: Orchestrator port (9 files, +456/-12 lines), completed in ~11 minutes (23:39→23:50), likely on a 40m timeout. 29+ minutes of dead time.\n- #122: Stale lock detected — agent may have completed or died, but without progress feedback, the operator can't tell.\n- This review session: stole a stale lock from pp3g-4gKW — that agent completed its review but the lock remained stale, suggesting the session ended without proper cleanup.\n\n**Recommended task-matched timeouts:**\n| Task type | Evidence | Recommended timeout |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trivial (1-2 file, <5 LOC changes) | #120: 2-3 min actual | 10m |\n| Documentation/port (<10 files) | #121: 11 min actual | 20m |\n| Review/audit (read-only) | This session: ~5 min setup + review | 15m |\n| Multi-file feature (>10 files) | No direct evidence, estimate | 30m |\n| Complex multi-phase | Swarm territory, not kickoff | 45m+ (use swarm, not kickoff) |\n\n**Key insight:** The 45m/40m/1h defaults are not just wasteful — they actively HIDE failures. A 40m timeout on a 3-minute task means the operator cannot distinguish 'working slowly' from 'dead agent' for 37 minutes. Shorter timeouts + checkpoint comments create a two-signal system: (1) timeout exceeded = likely stalled, (2) no checkpoint for >2x expected interval = likely stalled.\n\n---\n\n### Risk Analysis: Failure Modes\n\n1. **Agent non-compliance**: Agents may ignore template checkpoint instructions. This is the fundamental limitation of prompt-based enforcement. Mitigation: make checkpoints a crosslink-guard enforcement point (add to `tool.execute.before` hook — check for recent observation comments on active issue at commit time).\n\n2. **Comment noise**: Too many checkpoint comments dilute signal. Mitigation: enforce milestone-based (not time-based) checkpoints; limit to 4 max per session.\n\n3. **Sync latency**: Even with mandatory sync, there's a window between comment post and hub hydration. Mitigation: accept this as a known limitation; document the sync boundary in the operator contract.\n\n4. **Two-repo sync (#124)**: The issue mentions ASES and tripn-astro orchestrator roles must be updated together. This is a process risk, not a mechanism risk — but it means the checkpoint template must be synchronized across both repos.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n| Option | Role | Cost | Value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| (1) Checkpoint Comments | Primary operator-facing channel | Low (template + doc change) | High (visible, durable, auditable) |\n| (2) Session Actions | Supplementary telemetry | Very low (already exists) | Low (invisible to operator at hub) |\n| (3) KICKOFF Heartbeat | Enforcement scaffold for (1) | Medium (template rewrite) | Medium (compliance leverage, no transport) |\n\n**The timeout-length fix is prerequisite to any mechanism.** Shorter timeouts reduce maximum blind-wait time; checkpoint comments provide real-time progress visibility during the (now shorter) timeout window.","created_at":"2026-08-03T01:15:32.439049226Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:IIc89i12SLpCODNOhl1bmnXYF0W6MVtRNHNzZyizb2I","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgx55R2wNHtCGWUg+NJHz+OLWc5C0em3Z1T9MIOxyRHdoAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA/8ca6/F7ulLRkwt4u52gWlh4ahENPs3lH6ICLSFb9Nk3r6W5E03+dtjTcJSWFMYJXIGdUG7BZK5ikA/se0EtCw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:IIc89i12SLpCODNOhl1bmnXYF0W6MVtRNHNzZyizb2I","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgx55R2wNHtCGWUg+NJHz+OLWc5C0em3Z1T9MIOxyRHdoAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQFyukKWMznsjPo0MS+Y5OJnuVjGNfDvYslASBw++41swE9xTHhyRMrmMYiZRbMWKsH/z5lNIsv220QzjQDg79AE="}
